/-!
# Assembly of the two alternating-six reconstruction branches

`CubicPlaneResidualEnvelope` puts the full midpoint moving cover in the
derived subgroup of its selected residual envelope and proves the bound
|U_c| ≤ 2 |C_c'| |[Y,C_c]|. Its actual selector forces one of those two
residual commutators to have order greater than two. The same branch has
nonabelian intrinsic blockU L and U ≤ [Q,[Q,K]] in the original join.
Its residual upper bounds and terminal exclusion remain open.

`CubicPlaneDerivedCore` proves that a distance-four plane configuration
with uniformly small critical images forces the entire original moved
cover into the derived subgroup of the original two-core. It constructs
its strict slice and nonabelian selected span, transfers the derived line
to the midpoint-core intersection, and uses the actual cover representation
and midpoint embedding. Both covers and their central attachments remain.
The resulting lower bound does not establish the terminal contradiction
or either unconditional whole-core reconstruction.

CubicPlaneCommutatorFixedSeed identifies the fixed subgroup of the paired
radius commutator with the intersection of the two full last-edge seeds.
It is properly contained in the common full kernel and has index at least
eight in the endpoint seed, on one retained pair of literal cubic paths.

CubicPlaneDerivedAction splits the full endpoint seed into the common
kernel and the critical four-group. The commutator of the two starting
seeds fixes exactly that common kernel and moves exactly the four-group.
The paired-radius commutator contains a strictly larger endpoint-seed
subgroup, of order at least eight, on the same literal paths. Its terminal
exclusion and unconditional reconstruction remain open.

`CubicPlaneCommonKernel` computes the action on the intersection of the two
retained full kernels. In either radius, its normalizer equals its centralizer,
namely the first-edge orbit joined with the endpoint core intersection, of
index two. A radius conjugate of the common kernel recovers the entire own
kernel; their intersection is the opposite-kernel part of the last-edge seed.
Both literal path applications retain the full central attachments and both
covers. The paired exclusion and unconditional reconstruction remain open.

`CubicPlaneKernelSeparation` identifies the two distinct midpoint kernels
inside the same selected span. Under the two literal end-slice bounds, the
original switch carries one index-two kernel to the other; their intersection
has index four and they generate the span. Both retain its full derived line.
Endpoint control transports to the shifted kernel and fails on the original
one, so normalization of the first-edge join cannot replace the missing
shifted-path argument. Both generation identities, every path vertex and both
covers are retained. Source (5.12)(3)'s slice bounds remain to be proved.

`CubicPlaneTerminal` supplies opposite-core transfer from the literal small
forward slice, independently of point-core containment. In the original plane
branch, a midpoint-conjugate critical pair cannot have both midpoint slices
small. A nonabelian selected span constructs its own critical conjugate and
forces a strict reciprocal slice. Whole-core and literal C.1.32 applications
retain both covers and the original cubic kernel. The remaining selected-path
and small-slice source arguments, larger-distance and Borel exclusions, and
both final core exhaustions remain necessary.

`CubicPlaneEscape` rules out radius trapping in the original cubic plane
branch. It applies the shared alignment/control calculation to the same
selected endpoint chart; the retained escape excludes control, and the
aligned-displacement exclusion contradicts the same nonzero kernel movement.
Both full covers and the entire cubic kernel are preserved. Every critical
path now has backward walks with full overlap, a reverse prefix and forward
eligibility, without a large-image or strict-slice premise. Whole-core and
literal C.1.32 applications remove the finite-cover obstruction. The Borel
branch, the terminal plane configurations and both core exhaustions remain;
forward eligibility does not imply a strict slice.

`CubicPlanePredecessor` proves whole-stabilizer centrality and exact residual
transport for the full reverse-kernel commutator in a small predecessor slice.
One trapped radius discharges the slice premises on every critical path. Its
actual paired-selector application retains both generation identities and all
walk overlaps, and identifies a nonzero kernel line with the transported
endpoint movement. The comparison with the original critical line and endpoint
center, the Borel and terminal exclusions, and both core exhaustions remain.

`CubicPlaneRadius` shows that one trapped plane radius forces uniformly
small two-edge slices and exact full reverse-kernel identities on every
critical path. Conversely, one large critical image or one strict slice
supplies backward walks, reverse prefixes and forward eligibility on every
critical path. Its whole-core and C.1.32 consumers retain the uniform-small
plane case, the Borel branch and the terminal exclusions.

`CubicPlaneBackward` closes the trapped-pair alternative for the same original
plane selectors. The full trapped-radius calculation proves that the two
reverse-kernel commutators are conjugate lines. Generation by the selected
edge and its switched edge then makes the line central in the starting
stabilizer, contradicting joint movement greater than two. The actual
backward-shift consumer retains both generation identities, the original
switch and neighbor transporter, and every walk overlap. The Borel branch,
terminal exclusions and both final core exhaustions remain open.

`CubicPlaneKernels` identifies the two full critical kernels of the same
normalized selector pair: they are distinct and intersect in exactly the
first-edge Thompson fixed subgroup, of index four. Their two critical lines
generate a four-group inside that common kernel, which the original switch
normalizes. Every switched-path vertex and the strict-slice conjugator are
retained. The actual paired backward application excludes simultaneous
containment with both full reverse omega-kernel commutators central at the
eligible vertex. The noncentral reverse-kernel branch and the remaining
backward and terminal arguments are still required.

`CubicPlaneNormalizer` lifts image normalizers through the retained cubic
kernel and adjusts even lifts into the original component. On each unchanged
small critical path, the paired selector now normalizes the actual first-edge
Thompson join, and both original and switched endpoint seeds lie in that join.
Its quadratic action extends from the moving cover to the entire seed, so it
centralizes the paired endpoint commutator. Both cover orders and the original
projection are retained. These whole-core and literal C.1.32 applications
supply the normalized selectors; compatible backward paths and the remaining
plane/Borel contradictions, both core exhaustions and final reconstruction
still require proof.

`CubicPlaneFourSpans` proves that a large critical image at distance four
has an eligible original midpoint. In both remaining branches its controlled
even actor selects a generating backward path inside the original selected
span, proving that span nonabelian. The common-core index and forward
Baumann index then exceed two, by the actual central-quotient action and
even-lift displacement bound. The same transporter and every overlap vertex
are retained. The all-vertex whole-core and literal C.1.32 applications
discharge these strict-slice premises at distance four; terminal contradictions,
compatible small-image paths and larger-distance comparisons remain open.

`CubicPlaneSelectors` constructs paired source Thompson joins on every
small critical path in the remaining plane and Borel branches. Their join
is the original local stabilizer; one selected join together with the
critical seed and its conjugate generates the same group. The same even
component selector preserves the marked plane when the critical image lies
in it. Literal edge charts and no-supplement lifting retain the cubic kernel
and both full covers. `CubicPlaneLargeShifts` selects a generating edge
for the same controlled even subactor of a large critical path. Every strict
slice then gives a backward critical shift with the original conjugator and
all overlap vertices retained. Strict-slice existence, the remaining small
path choices, R4/R6 and terminal plane/Borel contradictions, both core
exhaustions and final reconstruction remain open.


The Schur-center dichotomy selects the natural or exceptional whole-core input.
In the natural case, irreducibility is derived from the actual natural action;
the exceptional case retains its order and faithful action. The resulting
reconstruction feeds the unchanged relative-block consumer of C.1.32.

Exceptional exhaustion is now proved for the faithful initial-action kernel
at every distance. At larger distance the actual moving central quotient
has one noncentral constituent: each nontrivial constituent costs at least
two dimensions, and the total critical displacement is at most two. Full
residual movement and zero fixed space then give an actual natural-four or
hexacode marking. The established derived-order and invariant-form
obstructions exclude both, so the distance is two and the entire intrinsic
core is exhausted. The literal rank-two consumer is applied without any
remaining faithful-radius premise.

The cubic initial-kernel branch with even natural image is also excluded.
Its original vertex quotient by Q is the actual triple cover, while the
initial natural action retains kernel E. The odd quotient E/Q changes no
two-subgroup displacement index. The radius therefore has the small
critical displacement budget and derived order at least four. Recognition
of its moving central quotient again gives the natural-four or hexacode
contradictions. The whole-core and literal rank-two alternatives retain
only the cubic full-symmetric natural action at larger distance. Its
exhaustion remains necessary; both distance-two kernels are already handled.
The full-symmetric branch now retains the actual natural or augmentation
cover of order sixteen or thirty-two, with its entire fixed attachment
central in the original vertex group. Its marked graph quotient retains
the cubic kernel, and every comparison radius image has order at most eight
modulo the actual two-core. The cubic chief-factor budget and an actual restricted reverse actor now
prove radius centrality at every component vertex, with no commutator-control
or parity premise left. The restriction preserves the complete critical
commutator on the augmentation cover; the natural cover needs no restriction.
The derived-radius and triple-commutator bounds follow on the original graph.
Under the source's large critical-image hypothesis, every chief series of the
literal radius quotient has exactly one noncentral factor. Nilpotent saturation
also proves that the residual fixes the two-kernel commutator modulo the full
seed. The whole-core and literal rank-two alternatives supply this complete
Section 5.2 family. Critical endpoint growth now bounds every radius index
below by twice its seed index. A large seed image forces radius index greater
than eight; a radius index at most four is exactly four with both seed indices
two and a noncentral critical line of order two. These estimates preserve the
cubic kernel and both central covers and feed the literal rank-two alternative.
The small critical kernel's pointwise fixer is now identified with the full
initial kernel joined to the opposite seed. The critical line and literal
kernel determine one another. A component conjugate gives a generating
kernel normalizer, and a seed element fixes the whole comparison path while
displacing that same kernel. These coordinated paths feed both the whole-core
and literal rank-two alternatives. In the large critical-image branch,
an actual escaping reverse subactor now constructs forward and backward
critical shifts at every prescribed incident edge. The same conjugators
transport their edges, every overlap vertex is identified, and the full
radius escapes both inner endpoint kernels. This holds for either cover
order with no remaining actor or displacement premise. The small-image
shifted-path exclusion, the R4/R6 calculations, and the distance-four
contradiction remain required. The full forward slice kernel is now normalized
by the starting stabilizer and contains the entire selected orbit span. A
small slice bounds the actual kernel image by two, retaining the cubic
kernel; its commutator lies in the common edge seed. Relative normal
generation controls both inner layers on the same critical path, and a
nonabelian selected span gives a noncentral line of order two at the second
vertex. The actual whole-core and rank-two applications retain the other
slice and span alternatives without a new universal source callback.
The small-image backward step now uses the fixed-space displacement bound
on either entire cubic cover. Point-core containment supplies the edge bound;
in the full point-core branch, perfectness lifts a generating edge across
the retained cubic kernel. The same conjugator preserves the prescribed
slice, first edge and all overlapping vertices. Both whole-core and literal
rank-two consumers are connected. Point-core identification, the remaining
source choices and strict slice selection are still required before the
R4/R6 and terminal contradictions.
Eligible neighbors are now constructed at every incident edge, and every
critical path has reciprocal slice index at least two. Two-subgroups of
the full seed centralizer lie in Q across the retained odd kernel; this
makes the forward-kernel commutator vanish exactly for a contained cover.
An eligible small forward slice consequently supplies a noncentral line
without assuming nonabelianness of the selected span. The whole-core and
literal rank-two applications retain the index-two and other slice cases;
the stronger index bound and terminal exclusions remain open.
The full cubic seed now has displacement coordinates under the original
symmetric projection, with centralizer E. Its generating-centralizer
choice proves that every small forward line is central at the start.
The entire selected span's commutator with the forward kernel equals that
line; in the nonabelian case it is also the whole derived subgroup.
The eligible-slice, whole-core and literal C.1.32 consumers retain both
covers and all remaining strict-slice and terminal exclusions.
The cubic midpoint calculation now identifies a small full-seed Baumann
slice with the reverse critical kernel, and the entire common two-kernel's
endpoint action with the critical line. A nonabelian selected span gives a
literal four-edge conjugate critical path; its whole derived subgroup is
that path's common-kernel commutator in the small branch. Above distance
four all selected spans are abelian. The strict slice and terminal
exclusions remain open; the original cubic kernel is unchanged.
Full reverse kernels now have predecessor-core commutator of order at
most two; a predecessor slice makes it residual-fixed, and midpoint edge
invariance makes it central. The same midpoint transporter identifies both
kernel commutators in both end-edge seeds. Full and moving reverse kernels
have identical predecessor-core commutators and equivalent slice placement.
The full predecessor Baumann slice now lies in the starting two-core:
a residual shift shortens the endpoint distance and fixes that slice modulo
the predecessor seed. A slice of index at most two is therefore exactly
the reverse critical kernel; both critical images have order two.
This discharges predecessor placement under full-point radius failure.
The actual residual-shifted large-image path contradicts the uniform small
slice bound, proving radius escape at every component vertex in the full
point-core branch. It also constructs backward critical walks with exact
overlaps. Large critical images have strict reciprocal slices independently
of point-core equality. Other radical-image cases, the small-image strict
slice branch, R4/R6 and the terminal exclusions remain open.
A small eligible slice in the full point-core branch now supplies the actual
forward endpoint and residual conjugator from MS93 (5.9), equation (**).
The same conjugator transports its entire orbit span and kernel; their
commutator is the transported order-two line. Above distance four, the span
centralizes the predecessor seed and full reverse kernel, lies in the
predecessor two-core, and still escapes its shifted Baumann slice. The
shifted-critical alternative is excluded, and both full orbits under
the line centralizer now lie in the old endpoint's two-core. The seed orbit
remains in the second radius and escapes its Baumann slice; the line centralizer
and the new endpoint seed generate the second local group. The cubic
kernel and both covers are retained. The orbit now has nonzero endpoint displacement modulo the second seed.
In the small shifted-image branch, its commutator with the full reverse
kernel has order two and is realized by one literal conjugated seed.
A restricted actor now excludes large shifted images for both covers:
both shifted indices equal two. The orbit commutator equals the full
